MITTRE ATT@CK Journey 

Tactic: Persistence (TA0003)

🎯 What is Persistence?

Persistence means:
    ["The attacker wants to maintain access to the compromised system even after the victim reboots, logs off, or changes their password."]

Think of it like this:

An attacker breaks into a house and secretly makes a duplicate key before leaving. Even if the front door is locked later, they can still come back.
That's exactly what persistence is in cybersecurity.


🧠 Why do attackers use Persistence?
If the victim:
Restarts the computer ✅
Logs out ✅
Closes the malware ✅
The attacker doesn't want to lose access.

So they create a method that automatically gives them access again.


| Technique              | Example                                           |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Registry Run Keys      | Malware starts automatically after login          |
| Startup Folder         | Malicious program launches at startup             |
| Scheduled Tasks        | Windows runs malware at a specific time           |
| Windows Services       | Creates a fake service that starts automatically  |
| New User Account       | Creates a hidden administrator account            |
| WMI Event Subscription | Executes malware when an event occurs             |
| DLL Hijacking          | Loads a malicious DLL instead of a legitimate one |



As a SOC Analyst, What Should You Investigate?

If you see an alert, ask these questions:

✅ Who created the persistence?

✅ When was it created?

✅ What process created it?

✅ Which user account was involved?

✅ Is it legitimate or suspicious?

✅ Is it signed by Microsoft or another trusted vendor?

✅ Did it communicate with an external IP afterward?


Example Investigation

Suppose you see this command:

  => schtasks /create /sc onlogon /tn Update /tr C:\Users\Public\update.exe

A SOC analyst would think:

❓ Why was a scheduled task created?
❓ Who executed this command?
❓ Is update.exe a legitimate Windows file?
❓ Does it exist in a suspicious location like C:\Users\Public\?
❓ Was it downloaded from the internet?
❓ What happened before and after this activity?


Common Windows Event IDs

These are very useful during persistence investigations:

4698 → Scheduled task created
4699 → Scheduled task deleted
7045 → New Windows service installed
4720 → New user account created
4732 → User added to a privileged/local group
4657 → Registry value modified (if auditing is enabled)

